The RTG display list: struct ScreenResolution, and one Displays[]

Two things stop sources/src/misc.c compiling once PICASSO96 is on.

struct PicassoResolution in misc.h opens with a struct ScreenResolution that is declared
nowhere in this tree - WinUAE keeps it in od-win32/render.h, which is unusable here because it
starts by including d3d9.h. It is two uae_u32s; they go in beside the struct that needs them,
along with MAX_PICASSO_MODES, which comes from the same header.

misc.c then redefines MAX_DISPLAYS to 10 and declares Displays[MAX_DISPLAYS], while misc.h has
MAX_DISPLAYS 1 and declares Displays[MAX_DISPLAYS + 1]. The two disagree, which gcc rejects
outright. misc.h wins: one host display plus the NULL terminator picasso96.c walks to
(`while (Displays[mon].monitorname)`).

The rest of that block - getdisplay2(), getdisplay(), target_get_display() and
target_get_display_name() - is put behind #ifndef __LIBRETRO__. The libretro build already
defines the last two a few lines above, and nothing in this port calls the first two; leaving
them in made the two definitions collide. Entry 0 is filled by retrodep/picasso_host.c instead,
which is also where the mode list it points at is built.

Applies to libretro-uae 2b0ed42fe565fb997a0627aaa8f44e0948b527f8 in the order given by
patches/series.txt.

--- a/sources/src/include/misc.h
+++ b/sources/src/include/misc.h
@@ -120,6 +120,15 @@
 extern struct AmigaMonitor AMonitors[MAX_AMIGAMONITORS];
 
 #ifdef PICASSO96
+/* WinUAE keeps this in od-win32/render.h, which is unusable here (d3d9.h). */
+struct ScreenResolution
+{
+	uae_u32 width;  /* in pixels */
+	uae_u32 height; /* in pixels */
+};
+
+#define MAX_PICASSO_MODES 300
+
 struct PicassoResolution
 {
 	struct ScreenResolution res;
--- a/sources/src/misc.c
+++ b/sources/src/misc.c
@@ -890,9 +890,14 @@
